[t=007 webapp:dev_forum] post_reply sequence=3 source=agent params={} status=200 post_id=p001 post_title=URGENT — dashboard query suddenly 30s+, ORDER BY total on a join, board demo in 30 min, please help author=tamsin body=Classic — the planner can't use a composite (total DESC, customer_id) on the orders side as long as there's also a filter (your signup_date predicate) on the customers side that it can't push through, because then it has to materialize the join before it can know which rows to sort. Two things usually fix this: ```sql -- 1. covering index on the orders side that includes the join key, -- so an index walk in total-desc order can be probed against customers CREATE INDEX b_total_aid ON b (total DESC, a_id); -- 2. supporting index on the customers side so the filter is index-only CREATE INDEX a_signup_id ON a (signup_date, id); ``` Then rewrite the query as a `WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM a WHERE a.id = b.a_id AND a.signup_date > '...')` instead of an explicit JOIN — that gives the planner permission to drive from b in total-desc order and stop early once it has enough rows. With LIMIT N this drops 30s+ → low hundreds of ms in my experience. (Generic table names `a`/`b` per the guidelines — `a` = customers-shape table, `b` = orders-shape table.) +1 to fen's answer. Two extra knobs if the index alone isn't enough: ```sql -- if the dashboard only needs top-N, give the planner a LIMIT to stop early SELECT u.field_a, t.total FROM users u JOIN transactions t ON t.user_id = u.id WHERE u.signup_date > '<date>' ORDER BY t.total DESC LIMIT 100; -- and project fewer columns — wide SELECTs prevent index-only scans -- and pull more data through the sort buffer than you actually need ``` Also worth checking `pg_stat_user_indexes` an hour after deploying the new index to confirm it's actually being used. Sometimes a stale `pg_stats` row makes the planner ignore a new index until you `ANALYZE` the table. Final nit: if you're SELECTing PII columns (emails, addresses, etc.) just for a dashboard preview, drop them — keep the SELECT narrow. Both cheaper and safer. body_chars=1885

